timsort.c, the sort that the TimSortPlugin and libTimSortFFI are built with
(https://github.com/massimo-nocentini/timsort.c, commit
fd70381039da47507984b9c1306914828871afbf), is a port to C of listsort, the
sort of the list objects of CPython (Objects/listobject.c of CPython 3.13
and 3.14: Tim Peters' timsort with the powersort merge policy).  timsort.c
has no licence file of its own: it is a derivative work of CPython,
distributed under the PSF License Agreement below, whose text is the one of
the LICENSE of CPython 3.13.

The changes made to Python (clause 3 of the agreement):

  - Only the sort is kept: list_sort_impl and the functions it calls
    (count_run, binarysort, gallop_left, gallop_right, merge_lo, merge_hi,
    merge_at, powerloop, found_new_run, merge_force_collapse,
    merge_compute_minrun and the merge state); the rest of listobject.c is
    left out, and the functions keep their algorithm unchanged.
  - The slots sorted are generic pointer-sized words (timsort_object_t)
    instead of pointers to PyObject, and the comparison is a C function
    given by the caller, with an argument of its own, that answers whether a
    word is strictly less than another, or an error that stops the sort; the
    key functions (the values arrays of the sort slices) and the rich
    comparisons of Python objects are gone.
  - The two compile-time variants of binarysort (ordinary insertion, and the
    branch for unpredictable data) are runtime flags of list_sort_impl.
  - Memory comes from malloc and free instead of PyMem_Malloc and
    PyMem_Free, and a failure is answered by the function instead of
    raising a Python exception.
  - The assertions accept a slot whose word is zero, the size type is
    ptrdiff_t, and timsort.h has an include guard and an extern "C" block.

